"""Hourly PV capacity factor for Hornsund from 3-hourly horizontal irradiation data.

Input format (as in Insol_3hrl_HOR_2015-2016.csv):
    line 1: site metadata, e.g. "Hornsund 76.966666 N  15.716666 E"
    line 2: Date, Global AllSky, Diffuse AllSky, Direct AllSky
    following lines: 3-hourly values in W/m², timestamps in UTC.

IMPORTANT INTERPRETATION
------------------------
The input file satisfies, row by row:

    Global = Diffuse + Direct

and is labelled HOR (horizontal). Therefore this script interprets:

    Global  -> GHI  = Global Horizontal Irradiance
    Diffuse -> DHI  = Diffuse Horizontal Irradiance
    Direct  -> BHI  = Beam/Direct Horizontal Irradiance

NOT as DNI.

poa= plane of array (è quello che nello scorso script era pv)

The direct component on the tilted PV plane is obtained from:

    BHI = DNI * cos(theta_z)
    POA_direct = DNI * cos(theta_i)

therefore:

    POA_direct = BHI * cos(theta_i) / cos(theta_z)

when the Sun is above the horizon and the front side of the module is illuminated.

The model then adds isotropic diffuse and ground-reflected irradiance:

    POA_diffuse   = DHI * (1 + cos(beta)) / 2
    POA_reflected = GHI * albedo(t) * (1 - cos(beta)) / 2
    POA_global    = POA_direct + POA_diffuse + POA_reflected

GROUND ALBEDO
-------------
The GUI asks only for a minimum and maximum ground albedo.

Instead of using arbitrary seasonal transition dates, rho(t) is linked to the
observed 2015 Hornsund/Fuglebekken snow-cover evolution used as a reference:

    17 May  -> SCE = 0.90
    10 Jun  -> SCE = 0.75
    15 Jun  -> SCE = 0.50
    20 Jun  -> SCE = 0.25
    27 Jun  -> SCE = 0.10
    06 Jul  -> SCE = 0.02

The ground albedo is then calculated from:

    rho(t) = rho_min + SCE(t) * (rho_max - rho_min)

Between the listed dates SCE(t) is linearly interpolated. Before 17 May the
reference snow-cover fraction is set to 1.0 (fully snow-covered ground), while
from 06 July onward it is set to 0.0 (snow-free ground).

IMPORTANT: only the observed 2015 spring/summer snowmelt profile is used here.
No autumn snow-return dates are invented. Therefore the model keeps rho = rho_min
after 06 July. If autumn snow-cover observations are added later, this section
can be extended without changing the PV equations.

The irradiation CSV does not contain ambient temperature, so the script asks for a
separate SEKLIMA temperature file and applies the NOCT temperature correction.
"""

